(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-1-peripheral-cranial-nerves-quiz"]={"title":"Peripheral & Cranial Nerves — Anatomy Exam 1","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What two branches do all spinal nerves form?","choices":["Superior ramus and inferior ramus, which divide at each intervertebral level","Only a single ventral ramus, with no corresponding dorsal branch","Dorsal (posterior) ramus and ventral (anterior) ramus","White ramus communicans and gray ramus communicans only"],"answer":2,"exp":"All spinal nerves form two branches: the dorsal (posterior) ramus and the ventral (anterior) ramus. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which spinal nerves have two additional branches (white and gray rami), and what are these rami collectively called?","choices":["Spinal nerves S1 to S5; collectively called the sacral plexus","Spinal nerves C1 to C5; collectively called the cervical plexus","Spinal nerves T1 to L2; collectively called rami communicantes","All spinal nerves have these additional branches"],"answer":2,"exp":"Spinal nerves T1 to L2 have two additional branches — the white ramus and gray ramus — which are collectively called rami communicantes. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What are white and gray rami associated with, in the autonomic nervous system?","choices":["The autonomic ganglion of the parasympathetic division","The autonomic ganglion of the sympathetic division","The enteric nervous system exclusively","No association with the ANS"],"answer":1,"exp":"White and gray rami are associated with the autonomic ganglion of the sympathetic division within the autonomic nervous system. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What are dermatomes?","choices":["A classification scheme used to categorize different reflex arc types","A specific branch given off by each of the twelve cranial nerves","Regions of skeletal muscle supplied by a given spinal nerve, called a myotome","Specific regions of the skin supplied by a given spinal nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"Spinal nerves supply specific regions of the skin known as dermatomes. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"In the distribution of motor fibers within a spinal nerve, what do the white ramus (preganglionic) and gray ramus (postganglionic) carry?","choices":["White ramus carries postganglionic fibers back to the spinal nerve; gray ramus carries preganglionic fibers to the sympathetic ganglion","White ramus carries somatic motor fibers to skeletal muscle; gray ramus carries somatic sensory fibers back to the spinal cord","White ramus carries parasympathetic fibers only; gray ramus carries no fibers at all, serving as a structural attachment","White ramus carries preganglionic sympathetic fibers to the sympathetic ganglion; gray ramus carries postganglionic fibers back to the spinal nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"The white ramus carries preganglionic fibers to the sympathetic ganglion, while the gray ramus carries postganglionic fibers back to the spinal nerve for distribution to smooth muscles, glands, and visceral organs. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"In the distribution of sensory fibers within a spinal nerve, what information does the dorsal ramus carry versus the ventral ramus?","choices":["Dorsal ramus carries sensory information from exteroceptors/proprioceptors/interoceptors of the back; ventral ramus carries the same from the body wall and limbs","Dorsal ramus carries only proprioceptive information from muscle spindles; ventral ramus carries only nociceptive information from visceral organs","Dorsal ramus carries motor information to the deep back muscles only; ventral ramus carries sensory information from the limbs only","Dorsal ramus carries only visceral sensory information from thoracic and abdominal organs; ventral ramus carries only somatic sensory information from the limbs"],"answer":0,"exp":"The dorsal ramus carries sensory information from exteroceptors, proprioceptors, and interoceptors of the back, while the ventral ramus carries sensory information from exteroceptors, proprioceptors, and interoceptors of the body wall and limbs. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is a nerve plexus, and what are the four major nerve plexuses?","choices":["A single unbranched peripheral nerve; the radial, ulnar, median, and femoral nerves are examples","Only two plexuses exist in the body: the cervical plexus and the lumbar plexus","A type of autonomic ganglion found only among the twelve cranial nerves","An interwoven network of nerves (\"plexus\" means \"to braid\"); the cervical, brachial, lumbar, and sacral plexuses"],"answer":3,"exp":"A nerve plexus is an interwoven network of nerves (\"plexus\" meaning \"to braid\"); there are four nerve plexuses — cervical, brachial, lumbar, and sacral (the lumbar and sacral are sometimes combined into the lumbosacral plexus). — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What spinal nerve levels form the cervical plexus, and what does it innervate?","choices":["C5-T1; innervates the muscles of the shoulder and upper limb","C1-C5; innervates the muscles of the neck and the diaphragm","T12-L4; innervates the pelvic girdle and anterior thigh","L4-S4; innervates the muscles of the lower limb and buttock"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cervical plexus (C1-C5) innervates the muscles of the neck and the diaphragm. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which nerve, arising from the cervical plexus, innervates the diaphragm?","choices":["Great auricular nerve, arising from the C2 and C3 spinal nerve levels","Ansa cervicalis, formed by the union of C1 through C3 spinal nerve fibers","Lesser occipital nerve, arising solely from the C2 spinal nerve level","Phrenic nerve (originating mainly from C4, with contributions from C3 and C5)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The phrenic nerve, which provides sensory information from and motor innervation to the diaphragm, originates from C4 with minor contributions from C3 and C5. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which nerve of the cervical plexus innervates five of the extrinsic laryngeal muscles, traveling with fibers from CN XII?","choices":["Phrenic nerve","Transverse cervical nerve","Ansa cervicalis","Great auricular nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ansa cervicalis originates from branches of C1-C3 (and sometimes C4) and travels inferiorly with fibers from cranial nerve XII, innervating five of the extrinsic laryngeal muscles. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What sensory territory does the great auricular nerve cover?","choices":["Skin over the parotid gland, posterior aspect of the ear, and skin of the neck","Skin of the neck and shoulder only, sparing the ear and parotid region","Skin of the anterior triangle of the neck only, sparing the ear region","Motor innervation to the rhomboid muscles and levator scapulae"],"answer":0,"exp":"The great auricular nerve arises from C2 and C3, crosses anteriorly to the sternocleidomastoid, and travels toward the parotid gland; it receives sensory information from the skin over the gland, the posterior aspect of the ear, and skin of the neck. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What spinal nerve levels form the brachial plexus, and what does it innervate?","choices":["T12-L4; innervates the pelvic girdle and anterior thigh","L4-S4; innervates the pelvic girdle and lower limb muscles","C5-T1; innervates the muscles of the pectoral girdles and upper limbs","C1-C5; innervates the muscles of the neck and diaphragm"],"answer":2,"exp":"The brachial plexus (C5-T1) innervates the muscles of the pectoral girdles and upper limbs. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the correct organizational sequence of the brachial plexus, from the spinal cord outward?","choices":["Nerves → cords (posterior/lateral/medial) → divisions (anterior/posterior) → trunks (superior/middle/inferior) → roots (ventral rami)","Ventral rami (roots) → trunks (superior/middle/inferior) → divisions (anterior/posterior) → cords (posterior/lateral/medial) → nerves","Divisions (anterior/posterior) → cords (posterior/lateral/medial) → roots (ventral rami) → trunks (superior/middle/inferior) → nerves","Trunks (superior/middle/inferior) → roots (ventral rami) → nerves → cords (posterior/lateral/medial) → divisions (anterior/posterior)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The brachial plexus is organized, from the spinal cord outward, as ventral rami (roots), then trunks (superior, middle, inferior), then divisions (anterior and posterior), then cords (posterior, lateral, medial), and finally nerves. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which four nerves are given as examples of nerves formed by the brachial plexus?","choices":["Femoral, obturator, sciatic, and pudendal nerves","Radial, ulnar, median, and musculocutaneous nerves","Phrenic, ansa cervicalis, lesser occipital, and great auricular nerves","Iliohypogastric, ilio-inguinal, genitofemoral, and lateral femoral cutaneous nerves"],"answer":1,"exp":"The brachial plexus forms nerves including the radial, ulnar, median, and musculocutaneous nerves. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What spinal levels make up the lumbar plexus and sacral plexus respectively, and what does the combined lumbosacral plexus innervate?","choices":["Lumbar plexus (T12-L4) and sacral plexus (L4-S4); together innervate the muscles of the pelvic girdle and lower limbs","Only a single combined plexus exists, spanning T12-S4, with no separate lumbar or sacral levels","Lumbar plexus (C5-T1) and sacral plexus (C1-C5); together innervate the upper limb and shoulder","Lumbar plexus (L4-S4) and sacral plexus (T12-L4); together innervate the neck and diaphragm"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lumbar plexus (T12-L4) and sacral plexus (L4-S4) together make up the lumbosacral plexus, which innervates the muscles of the pelvic girdle and lower limbs. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What do the iliohypogastric and ilio-inguinal nerves innervate?","choices":["The gracilis, obturator externus, and adductor magnus muscles","The quadriceps femoris, sartorius, and iliopsoas muscles","The external oblique, internal oblique, and transverse abdominis","The gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fasciae latae"],"answer":2,"exp":"The iliohypogastric nerve and ilio-inguinal nerve both innervate the external oblique, internal oblique, and transverse abdominis. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does the femoral nerve innervate?","choices":["External and internal oblique, and transverse abdominis","Gracilis, obturator externus, and adductor magnus","Quadriceps, sartorius, pectineus, and iliopsoas","Gluteus maximus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The femoral nerve innervates the quadriceps, sartorius, pectineus, and iliopsoas. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does the obturator nerve innervate?","choices":["Gluteus minimus, gluteus medius, and tensor fasciae latae","Semimembranosus, semitendinosus, and adductor magnus","Quadriceps, sartorius, and iliopsoas","Gracilis, obturator externus, and adductor magnus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The obturator nerve innervates the gracilis, obturator externus, and adductor magnus. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What sensory territory does the genitofemoral nerve cover?","choices":["Skin of the anteromedial surface of the thigh and external genitals","Skin of the neck and shoulder, per the supraclavicular nerve territory","Skin of the anterior, lateral, and posterior thigh, excluding genitals","Skin of the perineum, posterior thigh, and posterior leg"],"answer":0,"exp":"The genitofemoral nerve provides sensory information from the skin of the anteromedial surface of the thigh and the external genitals. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does the superior gluteal nerve innervate, versus the inferior gluteal nerve?","choices":["Superior gluteal: piriformis and obturator internus; Inferior gluteal: quadratus femoris","Superior gluteal: gluteus maximus and piriformis; Inferior gluteal: gluteus medius and minimus","Superior gluteal: gluteus minimus, gluteus medius, and tensor fasciae latae; Inferior gluteal: gluteus maximus","Superior gluteal: gluteus maximus only; Inferior gluteal: gluteus minimus, medius, and tensor fasciae latae"],"answer":2,"exp":"The superior gluteal nerve innervates the gluteus minimus, gluteus medius, and tensor fasciae latae, while the inferior gluteal nerve innervates the gluteus maximus. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What does the sciatic nerve innervate, per the material's listed muscles?","choices":["Gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fasciae latae","Gracilis, obturator externus, and adductor magnus muscles","Semimembranosus, semitendinosus, and adductor magnus","Quadriceps femoris, sartorius, and iliopsoas muscles"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sciatic nerve innervates the semimembranosus, semitendinosus, and adductor magnus. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What does the pudendal nerve innervate?","choices":["The diaphragm and phrenic-innervated abdominal structures","The quadriceps femoris, sartorius, and iliopsoas muscles","The perineum, external anal sphincter, and urethral sphincter","The gluteus maximus, piriformis, and obturator internus muscles only"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pudendal nerve innervates the perineum, external anal sphincter, and urethral sphincter. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What sensory territory does the posterior femoral cutaneous nerve cover?","choices":["The anterior, lateral, and posterior thigh, excluding the perineum","The perineum, posterior thigh, and posterior leg","The neck, posterior scalp, and posterior head","The anteromedial thigh and external genitals only"],"answer":1,"exp":"The posterior femoral cutaneous nerve provides sensory information from the perineum, posterior thigh, and posterior leg. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is a reflex, and what is a reflex arc?","choices":["A reflex arc begins at a motor neuron and ends at a sensory receptor; a reflex is a consciously planned, voluntary motor response requiring cortical input","A reflex is the neural \"wiring\" itself; a reflex arc is the resulting involuntary motor response produced once the circuit is activated","A reflex is a voluntary, consciously controlled motor response; a reflex arc is the neural wiring of multiple reflexes combined into one circuit","A reflex is an immediate involuntary motor response; a reflex arc is the neural \"wiring\" of a single reflex, beginning at a sensory receptor and ending at a peripheral effector"],"answer":3,"exp":"A reflex is an immediate involuntary motor response, and a reflex arc is the neural \"wiring\" of a single reflex, beginning at a sensory receptor and ending at a peripheral effector. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the correct pathway of a reflex arc, in order?","choices":["Activation of a motor neuron → information processing → activation of a sensory receptor → response by the effector → relay to the CNS","Information processing → response by the effector → activation of a sensory receptor → relay to the CNS → activation of a motor neuron","Activation of a sensory receptor → relay of information to the CNS → information processing → activation of a motor neuron → response by the effector","Response by the effector → activation of a sensory receptor → relay to the CNS → information processing → activation of a motor neuron"],"answer":2,"exp":"The reflex arc pathway is: activation of a sensory receptor, relay of information to the CNS, information processing, activation of a motor neuron, and response by the effector. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Reflexes are classified according to which four criteria, per the material?","choices":["Only their development, classified strictly as either innate (genetically programmed) or acquired (learned through practice)","Development (innate/acquired), site of processing (spinal/cranial), nature of motor response (somatic/visceral/autonomic), and complexity (monosynaptic/polysynaptic)","Only the specific muscle group involved, categorized by upper limb, lower limb, or axial muscles","Only their complexity, classified strictly as either monosynaptic or polysynaptic circuits"],"answer":1,"exp":"Reflexes are classified according to their development (innate or acquired), the site where information is processed (spinal or cranial/cerebral), the nature of the resulting motor response (somatic, visceral, or autonomic), and the complexity of the neural circuit (monosynaptic or polysynaptic). — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the difference between monosynaptic and polysynaptic spinal reflexes?","choices":["Monosynaptic involves a single segment of the spinal cord; polysynaptic integrates motor output from several spinal segments","Monosynaptic integrates several spinal segments; polysynaptic involves only a single spinal cord segment","Monosynaptic requires cortical processing; polysynaptic is processed entirely within the brainstem","Monosynaptic involves only cranial reflex circuits; polysynaptic involves only autonomic ganglia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Monosynaptic spinal reflexes involve a single segment of the spinal cord, while polysynaptic reflexes integrate motor output from several spinal segments. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the stretch reflex (e.g., the patellar reflex), and what is its pathway?","choices":["A stimulus compresses a tendon, activating a Golgi tendon organ; information is processed consciously in the cerebral cortex before the muscle relaxes","A stimulus compresses a muscle, directly activating a motor neuron with no sensory neuron or spinal processing involved","A stimulus stretches a muscle, activating a sensory neuron; information is processed in the spinal cord, motor neurons are activated, and the muscle (effector) contracts","A stimulus stretches a tendon, activating a Golgi tendon organ; this reflex occurs only in the upper limb, never the lower limb"],"answer":2,"exp":"In a stretch reflex, a stimulus stretches a muscle, activating a sensory neuron; information is processed in the spinal cord, motor neurons are activated, and the muscle (effector) contracts — the patellar reflex is a classic example, controlled by muscle spindles in the quadriceps femoris. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What role do higher centers play in reflexes, per the material?","choices":["Enhance or suppress spinal reflexes, and modify information carried in descending tracts of the spinal cord","Are the sole site of all reflex processing, entirely replacing the spinal cord's role in reflex circuits","Have no measurable influence on spinal reflexes or descending tract information","Only affect cranial nerve reflexes, never influencing spinal cord reflexes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Higher centers can enhance or suppress spinal reflexes and modify information carried in the descending tracts of the spinal cord. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What structures make up a peripheral nerve, and how does the anatomy relate to the nerve plexuses previously described?","choices":["Bundled axons (organized similarly to bundled muscle fibers), which combine within plexuses to form the named peripheral nerves like the radial, femoral, and sciatic nerves","Only neuron cell bodies clustered together into ganglia, with no axons, myelin, or connective tissue covering present at all","A bundled tract of white matter found exclusively within the central nervous system's spinal cord and brain, never in the periphery","A single unmyelinated axon traveling alone from spinal cord to target, with no connective tissue covering or bundling with other axons"],"answer":0,"exp":"A peripheral nerve consists of bundled axons, and within nerve plexuses, fibers from multiple spinal nerve roots combine and reorganize to form the named peripheral nerves (such as the radial, femoral, and sciatic nerves) that emerge from each plexus. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 7"}]};
